schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: Traditional remittances cost ~6% on average; stable-digital-dollar rails cost a cent or two
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Sending money home through a traditional money-transfer service costs
    about 6% of the amount on average worldwide (and often more), according
    to the World Bank, while a stable digital dollar sent on a low-cost
    network can cost a cent or two — a meaningful gap for diaspora
    communities and cross-border solidarity funds.
  evidence: >-
    Cited to the World Bank's Remittance Prices Worldwide dataset, linked in
    the article's references (remittanceprices.worldbank.org).
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/what-is-cryptocurrency.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used to make the "why this might matter for your work" case concrete —
    reframing crypto's cost advantage as relevant to real diaspora/solidarity
    cross-border transfers rather than abstract finance.
  uncertainty: >-
    "About 6%" is a global average masking large corridor-to-corridor
    variation; the "a cent or two" figure for stable-digital-dollar transfer
    is illustrative and network/fee-condition dependent, not sourced to a
    specific transaction example.
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-cryptocurrency.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
